                  </td>
                  <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">
                    Family, community, or existing relationships may play a stronger role
                  </td>
                  <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">
                    This does not mean every person has the same priorities
                  </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">
                    Mixed influences
                  </td>
                  <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">
                    A person may value both independence and close group relationships
                  </td>
                  <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">
                    Most real people cannot be reduced to one cultural category
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Practical takeaway:</strong> use cultural frameworks to become more aware, not
            to predict someone's personality. Ask the person what matters to them rather than
            assuming their nationality gives you the answer.
          </p>

          <h3>How Friendship Is Defined Differently</h3>

          <p>
            In some cultures, calling someone a “friend” may happen quickly after a few pleasant
            chats. In others, it takes time and consistency. Being aware of this helps you respect
            personal boundaries while building lasting connections.
          </p>

          <p>
            When I first started using this platform, I chatted with users from South Korea who
            initially seemed formal. But over several conversations, their warmth and humor
            emerged. They later told me that patience shows sincerity — a valuable cultural
            insight that reshaped how I view friendship. If you want to
            <a href="/blog/tips-for-practicing-a-new-language-with-native-speakers-online">
              talk to people around the world
            </a>, this guide can help.
          </p>

          <h2>Culture Influences People — But It Does Not Define Them</h2>

          <p>
            One of the easiest mistakes in cross-cultural friendship is replacing one stereotype
            with another. Learning that a cultural pattern exists does not mean that every
            individual follows it.
          </p>

